A||||5||doughboySE||02 senser||||1||
Z||000000||doughboySE||02-01-2000||10:40 PM||||is it very hard to replace a 02 senser?<BR>i noticed when i moved my car on sunday that im really burning rich and caron is alllllllllll over the pipes any suggestions?||||reg||1
Z||000001||David Gamache||02-01-2000||10:58 PM||||The 02 is easy. Make sure the manifold is warm to expand the metal a little and use a 02 socket sold at most parts places. Apply even pressure and remove old sensor.Remember to use anti-sieze on the threads of the new sensor(sensor should be replaced every 12,000 miles). Finally chech for cracks around the o-ring weld in the manifold. On old manifolds the pressure exerted to remove an old sensor can cause the welds around the 0-ring to fail. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||daved||02-01-2000||11:27 PM||||Doughboy, have you checked those codes yet?  You could glean valuable info from them. Code 13 or 44 will tell you the O2 sensor needs attention.  I try and measure the voltage output while its running. This will give you an idea if its doing its job. Use a digital voltmeter (cheap Canadian Tire type is o/k). Locate the sensor..it looks like a big sparkplug sticking out of the vertical exhaust pipe, drivers side almost by the firewall. Mind you dont burn yourself on the hot exhaust, and hook the red probe on the bare connection at the end of the sensor, and the black probe to a good ground. Start the engine, and watch the voltage rise to 400 to 800 mV..thats milliVolts, or about 0.8 of a volt (approximately)  The voltage varies with the amount of O2 in the exhaust. If its not somewhere in this range, then I would replace it.  Note that this sensor will only effect the mixture after the engine reaches operating temperature.  The ECM looks at its own sensor to tell engine temp., but see what you find with the o2 sensor first.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierospeeder||02-02-2000||12:44 AM||||if your on a budget, you can just snip the wire and put a socket on it and get it off.  If its the original oxygen sensor, you might have to put some muscle into it.  When unplugging the sensor, be careful with the plug, because it is brittle from the heat.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||02-02-2000||07:46 PM||||On the 4 cyl all you need is a box end wrench. just thread the wire thru it like a needle. you can install the same way. If you have room that's all you need on any car. problem is most cars burry the thing.<P>New sensors usually come with anti sieze on the threads. Don't remove it, and don't add to it. Don't put any anyplace but the threads. If you remove a sensor and reinstall it then use fresh anti sieze.<P>I prefer the Bosch sensors myself, they are a bit better shielded then some of the others. (important on the 4 cyl where things runn down off the deck lid when it's opened.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 02-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierospeeder||02-02-2000||11:18 PM||||I bought a trak auto oxygen sensor, and the cap on it where the wire was melted.  I bought one from the dealer.  Trak auto does carry bosch but you have to tell them you want the better one they sell.||||reg||1
